# <type>(<scope>): <imperative subject>
#
# Describe the changes using concise imperative bullet points.
#
# Why: <reason supplied by a person; omit for a trivial change>
#
# Types:
#   feat, fix, docs, refactor, chore, style, ci, test
#
# Scopes:
#   sass, includes, layouts, data, config, assets,
#   scripts, tests, ci, docs, agents, deps
#
# Add each applicable trailer as an uncommented line. All trailers sit in one
# final paragraph, after a blank line, one per line, with no blank line
# between them and nothing after them; Why: stays in the body above that
# paragraph. Repeat Assisted-by for multiple materially involved tools or
# models.
#
# Assisted-by: <tool>, <model id or not recorded> (<role>)
# Checks-run: <check actually run and its result>
# Ground-truth-source: <citation, or "n/a — property test">
#
# Roles: pick the first that fits.
#   full implementation: the tool wrote essentially all of the committed content.
#   partial implementation: the tool wrote part of it; a person wrote the rest.
#   refactor: the tool chose how to restructure existing content without
#     changing what it does or says.
#   plan: the tool proposed the approach or steps; a person wrote the content.
#   review: the tool reviewed or tested a person's work and wrote none of it.
#   transcription: a person wrote or fully specified the change; the tool
#     entered, moved, formatted, or committed it without adding content.
#
# Omit Checks-run when no check ran.
# Omit Ground-truth-source when no reference value changed.
# A property test does not require a Ground-truth-source trailer.
#
# Decide how to record prose assistance and document gaps in the history.
# Each commit lands on develop unchanged; keep it coherent.
# Do not infer or reconstruct trailers from memory; record only known provenance.
#
# Git uses this template only for editor-based commits when commit.template is set.
# git commit -m bypasses it. Review the final message.

